[
  {
    "url": "https://rec.github.io/safer/",
    "title": "safer`: A safer writer for files and streams",
    "snippet": "Feb 27, 2023 safer.printer() is safer.open() except that it yields a a function that prints to the stream. By default, safer buffers the written data in a io.StringIO or io.BytesIO. For very large files, safer.open() has a temp_file argument which writes the data to a temporary file on disk, which is moved over using os.rename if the operation completes successfully.",
    "published": null
  },
  {
    "url": "https://docs.rs/safe-write",
    "title": "safe_write - Rust - Docs.rs",
    "snippet": "A crate for safely writing files using an atomic write pattern. This crate implements a safe file writing strategy that helps prevent file corruption in case of system crashes or power failures. It follows these steps: Creates parent directories if they don’t exist Writes content to a temporary file Ensures the content is fully written to disk Atomically renames the temporary file to the ...",
    "published": null
  },
  {
    "url": "https://code.activestate.com/recipes/579097-safely-and-atomically-write-to-a-file/",
    "title": "Safely and atomically write to a file (Python recipe) - ActiveState",
    "snippet": "One approach to prevent this is to write to a temporary file, then only when you know the file has been written successfully, over-write the original. This function returns a context manager which can make this more convenient. Update: this now uses os.replace when available, and hopefully will work better on Windows.",
    "published": null
  },
  {
    "url": "https://okojj.github.io/ebook/books/perl/cookbook/ch07_09.htm",
    "title": "Recipe 7.8. Modifying a File in Place with Temporary File",
    "snippet": "Solution. Read from the original file, write changes to a temporary file, and then rename the temporary back to the originalNote that rename won't work across filesystems, so you should create your temporary file in the same directory as the file being modified. The truly paranoid programmer would lock the file during the update.",
    "published": null
  },
  {
    "url": "https://iifx.dev/en/articles/460341744/how-to-implement-atomic-file-operations-in-python-for-crash-safe-data-storage",
    "title": "temporary files - How to Implement Atomic File Operations in Python for Crash-Safe Data Storage",
    "snippet": "An atomic write means that the process of updating a file is seen as a single, indivisible operation by other programs or threads. If the writing process is interrupted (e.g., by a crash, power loss, or signal), the original file either remains completely unchanged, or the new file is fully and correctly in place.",
    "published": null
  },
  {
    "url": "https://dev.to/catatsuy/safely-updating-existing-files-in-go-1hlc",
    "title": "Safely Updating Existing Files in Go - DEV Community",
    "snippet": "If you want to safely update an existing file in Go, the basic rule is simple: do not write to the original file directly. Instead, write the new content to a temporary file first, and replace the original file with rename only after the write is fully complete.",
    "published": null
  },
  {
    "url": "https://docs.bswen.com/blog/2026-04-04-atomic-file-writing-python/",
    "title": "How to Implement Atomic File Writing in Python (No Partial Writes)",
    "snippet": "Learn the write-to-temp-then-rename pattern for crash-safe file operations in Python. Prevent corrupted files from partial writes during crashes or power failures.",
    "published": null
  },
  {
    "url": "https://stackoverflow.com/questions/1812115/how-to-safely-write-to-a-file",
    "title": "python - How to safely write to a file? - Stack Overflow",
    "snippet": "A better option would be to write to a temporary file somewhere, and when the write method has finished, you copy the temporary file to the original file. Now, if the copy somehow fails, you still have correctly saved data in the temporary file. And if the copy succeeds, you can remove the temporary file.",
    "published": null
  },
  {
    "url": "https://www.commandinline.com/shell-script-atomic-file-write/",
    "title": "Shell Script Atomic File Write Pattern Explained Step-by-Step",
    "snippet": "Shell Script Config File Guide Shell Script Production Template Wrapping Up The atomic file write pattern costs two extra lines — a mktemp call and a mv at the end — but prevents an entire category of silent data corruption bugs. Use it everywhere you write configuration, state, or any file that another process might read mid-write.",
    "published": null
  },
  {
    "url": "https://shelf.tylerbutler.com/advanced/persistence-operations/",
    "title": "Save, reload, sync, and close operations for managing data persistence.",
    "snippet": "The save uses a temp-file-plus-rename strategy for crash safety: data is written to a temporary file first, then atomically renamed over the original DETS file. If the process is killed mid-save, the original file remains intact until the rename succeeds. Internally, the ETS-to-DETS copy uses ets:to_dets/2, which transfers entries inside the Erlang VM without materializing the table as a list.",
    "published": null
  }
]
